@meta
id: 16-02
title: Go by name
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: buffer-switch, buffer-list
par: 90
minutes: 5
vscode: squint at six tabs, click the one that looks right
open: order.entity.ts order.provider.ts order.repository.ts customer.provider.ts customer.repository.ts errors.json

@brief
Six buffers. Numbers work, but you have to look them up first, and they
change from session to session. Names do not. `:b` takes a name as well
as a number, and it does not need the whole name: any unique fragment
will do. `:b entity` is enough. `:b ent` is enough. If the fragment
matches more than one buffer vim refuses with E93 and stays put, which
is the only thing that can go wrong.

Two fixes, in two of the six.

`order.provider.ts` throws `ORDER_MISSING` when an order is not found.
That code does not exist; `errors.json` has `ORDER_NOT_FOUND`. Fix the
provider.

`customer.repository.ts` maps `id` and `email` and forgets `name`. Add
`name: record.name,` directly under the `email` line.

There are two providers and two repositories open, so `:b prov` will not
do. Write before you leave each file; vim will not switch away from
unsaved changes yet, and 16-06 explains why.

@keys
| `:b ent` | go to the one buffer whose name contains `ent` |
| `:b order.p` | a fragment can include the dot; that is enough to tell two providers apart |
| `:b ord*rep` | `*` is a wildcard between two fragments |
| `:b ^cust` | `^` anchors the fragment to the start of the name |

@hints
`:b order.p` is the order provider and nothing else. `:b customer.r` is
the customer repository. Read the fragment aloud and check it cannot
mean two files.
--
In the provider, `/MISS` puts you on the bad word. `cw` from there
replaces `MISSING` because the underscore is part of the word; type
`NOT_FOUND` and it is done.
--
`:w` before the second `:b`. In the customer repository, `/email`,
`o`, type the mapping, `Esc`, `:wqa`.

@solution
    :b order.p<Enter>  /MISS<Enter>  cw NOT_FOUND <Esc>  :w<Enter>
    :b customer.r<Enter>  /email<Enter>  o name: record.name, <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

About eighty keystrokes for two files out of six, and no list, no
numbers, no looking.

This is the habit that replaces tab-hunting. You almost always know the
name of the file you want, or enough of it. `:b` plus three or four
letters plus Enter is faster than finding a tab with your eyes, and it
does not get slower as the list grows. The fragment matches anywhere in
the path, so `:b entity` finds `src/domain/order/order.entity.ts` in a
real project too.

When a fragment is ambiguous vim tells you rather than guessing, and
nothing has moved. Add a letter and try again, or use `^` to anchor to
the start, or `*` to join two fragments. `:b ord*rep` reads as "the one
with ord and then rep in it", which is how you would say it anyway.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_MISSING');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@start customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}

@start customer.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Customer as CustomerRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Customer } from './customer.entity';

@injectable()
export class CustomerRepository extends BaseRepository<CustomerRecord, Customer> {
  public toEntity(record: CustomerRecord): Customer {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      email: record.email,
    };
  }
}

@expected customer.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Customer as CustomerRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Customer } from './customer.entity';

@injectable()
export class CustomerRepository extends BaseRepository<CustomerRecord, Customer> {
  public toEntity(record: CustomerRecord): Customer {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      email: record.email,
      name: record.name,
    };
  }
}

